target:    tests/docker/verify-change.sh
gate:      composer test:seal-negative
kind:      restore-defect
expect:    T11 expected exit 1 and 'no packet built'
rationale: Restores the unguarded packet build. What only this entry can show is that the refusal has to live at the ENTRY POINT: seal-tool.py validates a packet and never the verdict that produced it, and the packet's own nine controls read the answers document, which an aborted run leaves complete and self-consistent. So every downstream check passes on evidence the harness has already refused. The measured shape of that failure is at verify-change.sh's own guard and at T11; it is not repeated here.
---
--- a/tests/docker/verify-change.sh
+++ b/tests/docker/verify-change.sh
@@ -107,9 +107,7 @@
 # and nothing downstream could tell the difference — seal-tool.py validates the packet, not the
 # verdict that produced it.
 ART="$WORK_ROOT/answers/answers/artifacts"
-if [ "$rc" != 0 ] && [ "$rc" != 2 ]; then
-  err "no packet built — the comparison aborted (exit $rc), so its captures are not evidence"
-elif [ -d "$ART" ]; then
+if [ -d "$ART" ]; then
   "$HERE/build-packet.sh" "$RUNS" "$ART" "$BEFORE_SHA" "$AFTER_SHA" || exit $?
 fi
 
